    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d88-a8">

                <pb facs="0252" n="118" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg118"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[lnclosure 8 in No. 538.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Leverich to Mr.
                            Hall.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-12-14"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 14,
                        1887</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: I beg to transcribe for your
                    information the following from a letter which I have received from this
                    company’s agent at Punta Arenas, dated 24th ultimo.</p>
                <p>“The 5 per cent question is still in the same shape, and certainly this
                    protection of <pb facs="0253" n="119" xml:id="pg119"/> Campo induces importers
                    to prefer this line, and more for articles like cigars, paying over $2 kilo
                    duty. The <hi rendition="#italic">Guatemala</hi> brought 80 tons of different
                    goods from San Francisco to here.”</p>
                <p>This will show you how handicapped this company is in meeting this competition as
                    long as this 5 per cent differential duty exists in favor of the Campo Line.</p>
                <p>Yours, truly,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. H. Leverich</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Special Agent.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
